former rangers everton and scotland  manager walter smith has died at the age  of 73 they'll be best remembered for his  achievements at ibrox where he won 21  trophies during two spells in charge and  led the club to the uefa cup final in  2008. rangers chairman douglas park  described him as much more than just a  football manager he said he was a leader  an ambassador and most of all a legend  let's speak to sky sports news reporter  mark benstead who's in glasgow for us  hello to you mark a sad day there  what have fans been reflecting on today  yeah good afternoon for my brock siri it  is a very very sad day and i think for  all connected with scottish football  particularly so if you're a  member of the rangers family if i should  call it that i think fans are out today  a little bit numb at the moment at the  news um still taking it in getting  getting it through their heads computing  yet some have already turned out here to  pay their tributes i think they're  reflecting on walter smith the man the  way in which he represented this  football club but also the huge success  he brought to it you touched on it there  were ten league titles six scottish  league cups five scottish cups  and the two spells in which he did help  bring success to the club first he came  in his assistant to graham soonest in  1986 he helped them win the club's first  league title in nearly a decade then  after not winning the title the  following year embarked them on this  fantastic journey to nine successive  championships when he came back in for  his second spell he came in when the  club's in a bit of disarray they'd had a  french manager polar glen it hadn't  worked he came back stabilized the club  and he subsequently won three more  championships as you say there was  success as well in europe he took into  that 2008 uefa cup file against senate  saints petersburg they almost got to a  champions league final as well in 1992  pipped in the group stages by eventual  winners marseille so they're reflecting  on all of that and i guess just what it  was like to have a man at the helm of  the football club who believed in the  club supported the club as much as them  it's not just the rangers fans of course  who will feel this one it's his former  players  we've tried to speak to a lot of his  former colleagues and his former squad  members and most of them are just in  bits  they are tearful they can't bring  themselves to come and speak to us  because they hit so hard  by the passing of walter i think  that kind of underlines the kind of guy  he was he had a stern outer outer front  you want to call it that he always  seemed quite abrupt sometimes sometimes  he's brought with guys like me in the  media but the guys who worked with him  and worked for him absolutely loved him  and this is hitting them very hard  indeed today it's also worth pointing  out he had  you know success elsewhere he had time  with the scottish national team who's  assistant to alex ferguson he woke up in  86 he was the manager in his own right  as well coming in after  the country was in a bit of a mess under  bertie vogels he made an impact though  but of belief and stability back to the  national side as well so his passing  today won't just be felt here at ibrox  it will be felt across the whole of  scotland  mark thank you
 

